{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjskyF76HfwePWn4S68","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about CI results for a lockfile bump, and they're concerned that the native addon build is using the layer cache. They want to know if a green CI run actually validates that the addon was rebuilt against the new ABI (application binary interface), or if it just means the cached layer was reused without actually rebuilding.\n\nThis is a valid concern: Docker layer caching (or similar build caching) can mask the fact that a step wasn't actually re-executed. If a layer cache is reused, it might mean the dependencies weren't actually rebuilt against new ABI requirements, even though the build succeeded.\n\nThe key question is: just because the build passed doesn't mean the actual rebuild happened if a cached layer was used. You need explicit evidence that the rebuild occurred.\n\nThis is a technical question about build systems and CI. The user is not asking me to do anything with code or tools - they're asking for advice/clarification. I should:\n\n1. Acknowledge the valid concern - cached layers can hide missing rebuilds\n2. Explain that green CI + cache reuse doesn't prove the addon was rebuilt\n3. Suggest ways to verify that the rebuild actually happened\n4. Keep it concise and direct\n\nThis doesn't require any tools or memory access.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6837,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6837},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"72640e8e-83d7-48ea-a613-feecb57edcb5","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:45:26.941Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjsjr2cVE